Usage:
  $SCRIPT_NAME init <owner>/<repository>         Set up a bare worktree repo in the current empty directory
                                          (a full git URL works too) and scaffold .wtree/config.json
  $SCRIPT_NAME add <worktree> [options]          Create a worktree. Checks out origin/<worktree> if it
                                          exists, otherwise branches off main/master
                                          and runs the pre-add / post-add hooks
      -c, --custom-branch <branch>        Base the new branch on <branch> instead
      -p, --print-path                    Print only the worktree path on stdout
          --no-hooks                      Skip both hooks for this run
  $SCRIPT_NAME remove <worktree> [options]       Remove a worktree and delete its local branch
      -f, --force                         Remove even if dirty / delete an unmerged branch
  $SCRIPT_NAME ls | list                         Show worktrees with branch, dirty and ahead/behind state
                                          (any extra args are passed to 'git worktree list')
  $SCRIPT_NAME sync [<worktree>] [options]       Fetch, then fast-forward the named worktree - or every
                                          worktree when no name is given
      -r, --rebase                        Rebase local commits instead of skipping diverged branches
  $SCRIPT_NAME sweep | clean [options]           Remove worktrees whose branch is merged or whose
                                          upstream is gone (asks first)
      -n, --dry-run                       Only show what would go
      -y, --yes                           Skip the confirmation
      -f, --force                         Include dirty worktrees and unmerged commits
  $SCRIPT_NAME path [<worktree>]                 Print a worktree's path, or the repo root
  $SCRIPT_NAME editor [<worktree>]               Open a worktree in your IDE, defaulting to the one
                                          you are standing in. Override the command with
                                          WTREE_EDITOR (default: webstorm)
  $SCRIPT_NAME run <worktree> <command...>       Run a command with the worktree as its working
                                          directory, e.g. 'run main npm install'. A single
                                          quoted argument goes through the shell, so
                                          'run main "npm ci && npm test"' works too
  $SCRIPT_NAME agent <worktree> [args...]        Run the coding agent with the worktree as its working
                                          directory. Trailing args are passed straight through.
                                          Override the command with WTREE_AGENT (default: claude)
      --model <model>                     Model to start with (default: opus, "" to omit)
      --effort <level>                    Effort level (default: xhigh, "" to omit)
                                          Both must precede <worktree>; anything after it is
                                          handed to the agent untouched
  $SCRIPT_NAME herdr-agent <worktree> [options]
                                          Start a named herdr agent on a worktree. Without a
                                          direction it uses an idle agent-free pane in this
                                          tab, falling back to the current pane
      -n, --name <agent_name>             Agent name (default: derived from the worktree)
      -k, --kind <kind>                   herdr agent kind (default: claude)
      --model <model>                     Model to start with (default: opus, "" to omit)
      --effort <level>                    Effort level (default: xhigh, "" to omit)
